A||||28||William||Need a pep talk||||1||
Z||000000||William||04-06-2001||02:10 AM||||Guy's, i'm pretty down on Fieros right now. I got the front of the car on jackstands and the front end job half complete, but have completely lost interest in the whole car. I no longer feel like finishing the job but it's got to be done. Here's the deal..... I was kickin butt on this entire front end rebuild. I've never done anything like this but it all seemed easy and all was going well until I got to the #$%## poly. As posted before I had fitment problems. After being pressed in, the bushings became longer then the inner sleeves and I felt that this would cause problems. (I've read a few posts about fieros having no suspension deflection after poly installs). Then I noticed that the inner sleeves had to be beaten into the bushings because the inner holes had shruken due to the compression of the bushings. I spend $20 on a 5/8 drill bit and reamed out the inner holes so that the sleeves could be installed without using a sledge hammer and having all the grease getting squeezed out. The bit made the holes pretty rough and 2 got slightly egged on one end(although there seems to be no play) The arms and spindles are already back on but now I'm in fear of the whole thing needing to be pulled apart and redone. Even if I did that, I would have the same problems with new poly. I'm lost to why there are 0 posts on the forum about these issues. Did everyone just ignore all these issues and just install them as is? Or am I the only person on the whole list that even had the problems? I just don't know. Hardly anyone replyed to my post.....Some had a problem with the upper a arm washers fitting but there's o posts, (forum search) about these other problems I ran into. So, now the car just sits on the stands while the neihboors leer at it. I feel like I just wasted almost a thousand bucks to make the car worse than it was, thats always nice isn't it?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Neverfinished||04-06-2001||02:21 AM||||I hate not getting any replies on here, but I think that if anyone had any advice they would give it right away. Sorry, I would help you if I knew anything about this topic.<BR>You know what they say;<BR>ITS ALWAYS SOMETHIN     $$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$<p>[This message has been edited by Neverfinished (edited 04-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Pontiaddict||04-06-2001||02:59 AM||||It sounds like you just got a bad set of bushings.<P>The only major problem I had was not greasing everything enough. (Besides the bolts being rusted to whatever they were attached to, and the alignment shops turning me away, and the stupid locking lug nuts....)||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||04-06-2001||06:19 AM||||I kind of think your mistake may have been in trying to hammer rubber parts. Even poly doesn't realy respond great to that. You might have been better off to invest that $20 bucks in a big "C" clamp, and use it like a press to push the bushings and sleeves into place. Or to take the arms to a machine shop and have them insert the parts. As for the bushings being longer than the sleeves, I don't know how much longer they could have been, I doubt poly would change shape that much. Sounds like they just weren't pressed in all the way. I don't know how short the sleeves were once everything was installed, but my guess is the bushings would have seated once you installed the arms and bolted them down. Hope the rest goes smoother for ya.||||reg||1
Z||000004||hugh||04-06-2001||07:06 AM||||How did you spend almost $1000?Did you buy aftermarket front end parts(Upper,Lower control arms)?If so where?I've had a few problems with the front end I put on my 88.||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||04-06-2001||07:26 AM||||Your gonna always have some kind of problems whenever you stray from stock OEM parts. Thats the reason I left my chassis almost all completely alone, along with brakes and a bolt in engine swap. I like the reliability. 90% if what I changed from stock was just cosmetic.||||reg||1
Z||000006||William||04-06-2001||09:10 AM||||Nothing was hammered in! they were pressed in. I was infering that it would have taken a big hammer to get those inner sleeves in, but one was not used.  Also, the bushings only became about 1 or 2 mm longer. The inner sleeves could not be pushed in or rotated by hand as was posible before the bushings were pressed in. I must explain things poorly. I'm thinking that perhaps this happened to everone but no one really noticed. I fly radio control helicopters, and everythig on those has to fit perfect with all the tolerances being exact so maybe I was just too anal about the fit??? I just don't see how every bushing could be bad and everybody elses was fine. Prothane told me that the Fiero front poly kit is being redesigned because of the bad fit. <P><BR>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Taijiguy:<BR><B>I kind of think your mistake may have been in trying to hammer rubber parts. Even poly doesn't realy respond great to that. You might have been better off to invest that $20 bucks in a big "C" clamp, and use it like a press to push the bushings and sleeves into place. Or to take the arms to a machine shop and have them insert the parts. As for the bushings being longer than the sleeves, I don't know how much longer they could have been, I doubt poly would change shape that much. Sounds like they just weren't pressed in all the way. I don't know how short the sleeves were once everything was installed, but my guess is the bushings would have seated once you installed the arms and bolted them down. Hope the rest goes smoother for ya.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><p>[This message has been edited by William (edited 04-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cheever2||04-06-2001||09:14 AM||||I don't know nothin' about Fiero suspension, especially the front.<P>I'm just here to give you moral support.  Like you said, a pep talk.  <P>You got more guts than me, kid.  There... feels better, doesn't it?  Hang in there, and keep asking.  Once you get this licked people will be coming to you for advice.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000008||William||04-06-2001||09:17 AM||||Spending a grand was easy, extra tools, poly, balljoints, tierod ends, new rack, kyb shocks/ struts, braided brake lines, and all this for the rear too!<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by hugh:<BR><B>How did you spend almost $1000?Did you buy aftermarket front end parts(Upper,Lower control arms)?If so where?I've had a few problems with the front end I put on my 88.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Eric_PA||04-06-2001||09:31 AM||||William, I had the same darn problems. I just emailed you about it. My car was apart for over two weeks since I decided to redo my front end. I WOULD NEVER DO IT AGAIN! From the start the project was going down the tubes. Here are some of the problems I have encountered.<BR> 1) GA brakes rusted badly<BR> 2) Broke all 4 swaybar bushing bolts<BR> 3) found that my lower control arm was broke<BR> 4) The bolts for the control arm where worn too badly to reuse.<BR> 5) Those things where very hard to press out!<BR> 6) the inner sleeve was indeed very tight once the bushings where pressed in.<BR> 7) They where also too short, but mine where like that before I pressed them in.<BR>In all I wouldn't recommend this to someone who needs there Fiero the next day, cause it may be apart for a while.<BR>Here is a pic of the car the way it sat for over 2 weeks    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Mar27$16.jpg">  <P>Oops, just went back to read this and notice you are looking for a pep talk and not negativity. Don't give up on it, I felt down when I did mine, and just about done. Just think about how great it will be to drive it again  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Eric<BR>86.5 GT Auto going 3800 5 speed <P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Eric_PA (edited 04-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfixit58||04-06-2001||02:15 PM||||William,<P>I take it this was your first MAJOR project and that your car is also a daily driver. Some of the most exasperating project I've ever undertaken were on my daily driver. Your ALWAYS in a rush to -- just get it done!<P>By your note, your feeling or (maybe imagining) pressure from your neighbors and others (including yourself) and it's making the project that much worse. <P>Now your stuck. You don't have the car to use and you don't have to ambition or enthusiasm for the project you had when it started. But, you can't stop even if you wanted to because you feel you'd have to just give it away just to get it out of your driveway.<P>Does that about cover it? Well, BEEN THERE DONE THAT! So, don't feel like your alone. The good news is, it gets better. You'll get through this and, even if it has to be done again, it won't be near as tough the next time. Why? because you've learned. Even if the only thing you've learned is how to stick with a tough project until it's over, you've learned. It called "intestinal fortitude" or more commonly: "GUTS". <P>I see that common trate in all Fiero owners. If for no other reason then it takes guts to drive a Fiero when everyone around you thinks your NUTS for even liking them. It takes guts to put up with anal statements like, "You actually drive that fire trap?" or "What's the matter... couldn't afford a Corvette?". <P>Yea, guts, that's what REAL Fiero owners have. And, if you make it through this and are able to sit behind the wheel and hang that first corner, you'll have a smile from ear to ear. And you'll be able to say with pride... I did it... I'm truly a Fiero man... I got guts!<P>Now,... go get you some GUTS!<P>Feel better?<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||Uaana||04-06-2001||02:31 PM||||Hell Ya!! What mrfixit said!<BR>Now pull up those boot straps and maybe even threaten your car a bit..Tell it you're going to trade it in for a Hyundai.. and when all else fails hit one of the broken parts you already have off the car really hard.<BR>Love to help you more directly but I get confused putting on sway bars..||||reg||1
Z||000012||Oreif||04-06-2001||02:34 PM||||It took me 3 weeks to get my suspension together. As for the poly's I just pressed the poly's into the sleeves until they were even with the inside edge of the control arm. (Note the inside sleeves were already in the bushing before I pressed them in) I then got new bolts and nuts from GM, Installed all the washers as required and then torqued everything in. I have done 3 cars like this and have driven mine for a year so far and no problems (except the occasional creak on cold mornings)||||reg||1
Z||000013||mrfixit58||04-07-2001||01:09 PM||||Feeling better, Wiliam?<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000014||Jake_Dragon||04-07-2001||06:44 PM||||GET YOUR BUT OUT OF THAT CHAIR AND FIX YOUR CAR  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||8
Z||000015||rogergarrison||04-07-2001||08:02 PM||||a. get more beer<P>b. 'when' it is done itll be more fun.||||reg||1
Z||000016||85Josh||04-07-2001||09:52 PM||||What rogergarrison said.<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
Z||000017||BAM-BAM||04-07-2001||10:58 PM||||  William, What you are experiencing is simple lack of knowledge. Now I may bust your balls a little bit here, but bare with me. First off, you should have done an exstensive search on the subject through the forum archives, they are a great wealth of info. You would have learned where others have failed and succeded. The second mistake you made was in trying to press in the bushings without the center sleve. Others may say that is the way to do it, but I had no problems with the sleve in. As for the bushing lengthening out,  read the previous statement. With nothing in the center to hold the shap, the bushing was alowd to distort and effectively elongate. A little extra on the ends is common. <P>   I had never worked on anything like this before in my life. I changed my entire front end except fot the rack. New ball joints, news tierods, new  polys, KYB's and Eibachs. I first conducted an exhaustive search and question campaign about my endeavor. I then took everything one step at a time. I didn't move on to another step untill the current step was completed. It all went smooth with the help of the Forum. You have'nt experienced anything that any other car owner that works on their own vehical hasn't. Consider this a lesson and start over. Your new poly bushings are trash (you drilled them)dump em. get new bushings and start over. E-Mail me for details on an effortless way to get this done. Not really effortless, but smooth. Remember I am a reluctant mechanic, not an expert. But, by bein as such, I have learned to make things easier and relatively clean. I really hate to work on cars, but I love to play with my Fiero. Try looking at it like it's a game and have some fun. Drop me a note and I'll cheer you up a little. Alls not lost, you are just experiencing technical difficulties.<P>Peace, B2 OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!||||reg||1
Z||000018||William||04-07-2001||11:17 PM||||Actually, I spent about 6 month researching this job. After having the fitment problem, I went back and reviewed EVERY past forum post on poly. There was No mention of streching poly or tight inner sleeves. Do a search and see. I feel the inner holes really needed reaming. They were so tight that they would not rotate. The bushings would try to rotate in the cups before the inner sleeve would turn. The arms and spindles are back on and I don't think I'm gonna redo the entire job again. I really don't think it was a lack of knoweledge but rather piss poor products. How many ways can you press in a bushing? Prothane told me that the front end kit fits badly and offered a free new kit when released.<p>[This message has been edited by William (edited 04-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||wkayl||04-08-2001||01:24 AM||||Look at it this way.  Almost everything I have learned about cars was from what I screwed up and had to redo.  My dad bought me a 53 Ford at a farm auction and another from a junk yard for parts.  After leaving home I worked at a small mechanics shop and eventualy a Ford dealer as a tune-up and electrical technician.(this was in the mid 70's when things wern't so complicated).  Consider this front end job as a learning experience.  When you get it back together it will still have been cheaper than farming it out and when anyone asks how to do it you will be able to be the first one to give real advice to the do and don'ts of the job.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/outline.jpg">     <BR>86GT original paint, interior, engine  <A HREF="http://www.fierowayne.homestead.com/fierowayne.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierowayne.homestead.com/fierowayne.html</A>||||reg||1
Z||000020||BAM-BAM||04-08-2001||09:53 AM||||  William, I thik you may have some issues with the bushings that you drilled. The center sleve is not meant to spin in bushing. The center sleve is a buffer between the pivot bolt and the urethane. There is a tendancy for polys to squeek a bit due to inadiqate lubication between the metal and the urethane. I belive what you have done is created an enviroment that is ultra conducive to "poly-squeel". Like I mentioned before, trash them, you already did that when you drilled them. I'm not bashing you, I am simply trying to help you avoid any real and extreme frustration. If you get stuck on something, poll the forum, you'll be amazed at the help you receive.<P> Peace, Just a brotha lookin out! B2  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Knowledge is POWER!<BR>That is why the ignorant remain Weak!||||reg||12
Z||000021||Taijiguy||04-08-2001||10:17 AM||||I have to agree about getting new bushings, maybe not so much on the element of squeeking, but in that since they have been drilled, and you mentioned the holes aren't really round, you've created play in the businhings, which is going to make them sloppy. They will break in, and you'll end up right back here doing this again, because it won't hold an alignment.<BR>As has been said, the sleeves aren't meant to spin or turn....how many of the sleeves in your rubber bushings turned when you yanked them? None I bet. Also, I'm going to throw this out, since I haven't seen it mentioned here: Do not preload the bushings, meaning: make sure when you tighten everything down, that they are supporting the weight of the car. Place a jackstand under the lower ball joint,or as far out on the assembly as you can safely get, and let the weight of the car on it, so everything sits in it's "normal" static position when you tighten all bushings down. <BR>Oh, and yeah, what MrFixit said too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Good luck~||||reg||1
Z||000022||Fierowrecker||04-08-2001||10:23 PM||||William!<BR>  Hate to say it, but it sounds like you got the wrong parts...<BR>I did the front and rear on my 84, no problems!<BR>Greased up the poly and with the inner sleaves in, I installed them in most cases by hand...<BR>Were your cups real clean, or did you leave a lot of rubber in them?<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000023||Tigger||04-09-2001||12:23 AM||||I read this thread before the weekend and lots of questions arised.  Well, just pressed in poly bushings in my RH upper and lower control arms today.  Took the inner sleeves out, lubed the outer of the bushing, pressed them in halfway by hand and drove them home by tapped them in with a hammer and block of wood.  Lubed the inner sleeve and tapped them in.  Now, I noticed that the bushing seemed to stretch, but only about 1/16" or so, not sure, but don't think it will be a problem when I go to install them.  Don't know what to say, maybe they sent you the wrong kit.  I wonder if it was for an 88' but not sure how much it be different?||||reg||1
Z||000024||WKDFIRO||04-09-2001||03:30 AM||||Im sorry to hear about your frustrations! The best I can tell you is how we used to replace the bushings at the shop and we never had any problems. <P>We would get a torch (not high just enough to get hot!) and toast those bushings until they either fell out or in some cases shot out, (always over a tub of water!) Its smoky, it smells but it really cleans out that opening! The bushings are then pushed in (by hand) and with a little tapping the steel inserts are put in. (after everything is lubed, inside and out, I had to go back in and do that!)bolt everything back up and drive away! Now, if the only problem is that you expected the sleeves to spin than you were only making more work for yourself! Yes a lot of the grease is going to be squeezed out but its not squeezed dry! <P>I say drive it. If it starts to feel loose than you already know what it is. You may not have done as much damage as you think. <P>Deflection in suspension I would imagine would be only a little stronger than when the rubber bushings were brand new!<P>I would agree on most things staying OEM. But in this case urethane is the way to go for suspension parts. Don't get discouraged it just sounds like you did a little bit more than you needed to do. If anything else you can look at all the posts and know that we have all been down that road (or under that car) and have at times thrown down the wrench and cursed out loud. <P>Its just like being in a relationship. Sometimes everything is happiness and joy and sometimes you gotta argue and fight.<P>Just be happy you weren't putting in aluminum bushings in! Before those were perfected they were a pain in the ass! You can now say that you intimately know the front end of your Fiero. Just take a breath and know that doing what you did, is beyond the ability of some people. And someone may have a question that you know the answer to because you have gone through this.<P>Do you think we all know as much as we do about Fieros because EVERYTHING we did went perfect? <P>Knowledge by way of bloodied knuckles and free flowing curses!<P>Welcome to the Forum, William!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>||||reg||6
Z||000025||William||04-10-2001||01:06 AM||||I would like to thank everyone for the responses. Because this subject has never been discussed on the forum, I feel it deserves additional exploration. Let me suit up in the flame retardant gear and look at this in more depth.<BR>I feel that some of the posts are in error. I'm no suspension expert, but I'm a pretty smart guy and can look at things logically. It was stated the the inner sleeves do not rotate within the bushing. I feel this asumption is dead wrong. Lets look at the lower a arm. As you torque the retaining bolt, the inner sleeve is being squeezed between the frame brakets. What you'll have is metal to metal contact, (the bracket face. and the edge of the inner sleeve), being pressed together with 60 ft lbs of torque. I don't believe you'll see any rotation there. The sleeve will be locked into place along with the bolt. The rotation is provided by the bushing spinning on the sleeve. The question is how tight can the inner sleeve fit within the bushing and still provide the needed delfection. A forum search will reveal posts in which some complained that the poly stiffened the suspension more than lowering springs, and some lost almost all deflection when pressing down on the hood. I believe that this could only be caused by the inability of the bushing to freely rotate on the sleeve. The poly should reduce slop and may transmit more vibration, but it should not stiffen the suspension that much. If it has, your poly is fitting too tightly and has locked up the suspension. I felt that the fit of my inner sleeves was so tight that I would incur these problems.<BR> Now, why have some had these issues and some haven't. The key is the post from FieroWreaker. He states that he was able to install the poly by hand. How tightly the poly fits into the outer cups will determine the fit of the sleeves. My sleeve to bushing fit and lenth was perfect before I installed them, (I installed them both with and without the sleeves inserted with no difference). But, it was a very tight press to get them in. If your bushings go in easily, your in good shape. If they go in with a struggle,they will deform slightly and the fit will suck. So why the variance in fit? My guess is that either the outer cup size varies form arm to arm or quality control at prothane sucks. I don't know which. <BR>What does all this mean? It means that I've spent way to much time this week thinking about poly. I've reinstalled the wheels and have taken the car off the jackstands. I've decided to take a break and rekindle my love for R/C helicopters. I'm sure i'll be back soon but I have gassed up the helis and for now i'm "GONE FLYING".||||reg||1
Z||000026||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||04-10-2001||03:58 AM||||The bushings make the suspension feel tighter because most of us have shot factory bushings in our 13 to 17 year old Fieros.<P>I put rear urethane control arm bushings in and though the sleeves could be pushed in with a screwdriver handle (no banging required), the suspension is stiffer now.<P>The rubber bushings' sleeves do not rotate freely.  Instead, the rubber is the pivot.  While this works, it will suffer from some degree of hysteresis.  The fancy word means that the rubber bushing does not twist on axis perfectly rising and returning in paths that are not necessarily the same.<P>The urethane bushings pivot around the sleeves instead.  The drag at this interface is less than the amount of torsion stored in a rubber bushing at the limit of travel.  And, the urethane gives the same resistance to rotation accross the range of travel.  <P>Last, the urethane bushings do not require torquing with the suspension at ride height.  This is because they are real pivots, not "sticks in bubble gum" like they installed at the Factory.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000027||WKDFIRO||04-11-2001||04:53 AM||||Lucky is correct.<P>The poly itself is what is pivoting. it was this surface that I had failed to lubricate when I put mine on. When I took them off I could see where they had rubbed. After I put on the grease I haven't had a problem since!<P>"hysteresis"<P>Way to go with the $1.50 word too!||||reg||14
Z||000028||Taijiguy||04-11-2001||08:34 AM||||My experience has always been to torque them at ride height, Personal choice I suppose. Might just be overkill to do it that way, but it makes me feel better. LOL<BR>William, I wonder if you've ever heard the expression "mechanical advantage". The long and short of it is how torque or force is mechanically multiplied. (more or less) There's alot more to it than I'm going to say here, but to address your concern about the sleeves moving within the bushings, you have to consider the mechanical advantage of tha "A" arms over their point of axis (the poly bushing) Do you really think it's possible that those bushings could be so tight inside those bushings as to hold over 800# <I>(wild assed guess at the weight on a front wheel)</I> basically, from their point of reference, 12" out? There's no possible way those sleeves won't move within the bushings. Yeah, they're tight. That's the whole idea!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR>Believe what you're being told here in all these posts. And believe this: The first time you get out in it after all this, and experience the fruits of your labor...well, maybe you might want to have some Kleenix handy...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>~Taiji<BR>||||reg||1
